---
description: Guard against leaking customer-identifying data (secrets, IPs, customer names) before commit/share
alwaysApply: true
---

Before committing, pushing, opening a PR, or writing content into a doc/export meant to
leave this machine, check the change for:

- Secrets/credentials (API keys, tokens, passwords, private key material)
- Public *and* private IPv4 addresses (except loopback `127.*` / `0.0.0.0`)
- Customer/company names — check `~/.claude/sensitive-data-guard-customers.txt` (exact
  match) *and* read the actual content for names not on that list (git remote, README,
  config, comments) — the list won't catch everything, context reasoning will
- Personal names/emails/phone numbers not on an internal allowlist domain

If something's found: for secrets/IPs/emails, redact in place with a generic
placeholder and say what you changed. For customer/personal names, either confirm the
replacement with the user first (normal commit flow) or, if the user explicitly asked
to scan-and-anonymize a whole repo/folder, anonymize directly with one consistent
placeholder per distinct name for the whole run (e.g. "Customer A", "Customer B") and
report a summary — don't stop per-instance in that mode.

Full detail: the `sensitive-data-guard` skill at `~/.agent/skills/sensitive-data-guard`
(symlinked into this tool's skills directory too). A machine-wide git pre-commit hook
also mechanically catches secrets/IPs/denylisted names on every `git commit`,
independent of this rule.
